# The pins of the OCSF check (`just ocsf-fetch`, docs/reference/approved-libraries.md "Tools that run in CI").
# Read by the recipe as shell variables. Nothing here is linked into a sluis artifact: it runs in CI and on a workstation.
#
# The OCSF version the example records are checked against (ADR 0030, "Each release pins one OCSF version"): the tag 1.9.0 of
# github.com/ocsf/ocsf-schema, pinned by commit. MEASURED 2026-11-07: `git ls-remote` and the GitHub API both give this commit
# for the tag; version.json in it says 1.9.0. Apache-2.0.
OCSF_SCHEMA_VERSION=1.9.0
OCSF_SCHEMA_COMMIT=856d462bd20dc46cc1ffed2dfffe3b91ef0fbeba
# The compiled schema is not pinned by digest: the compiler reads the schema directory in the order the filesystem lists
# it, so the same inputs give the same schema in another order of members on another host (MEASURED 2026-11-07: the digest
# on a workstation was not the one in CI). The inputs are pinned instead (the commit above, and the compiler by hash in
# tools/ocsf/requirements.txt), and `ocsf-fetch` checks that the compiled file says OCSF_SCHEMA_VERSION.
# The toolkit (github.com/ocsf/ocsf-toolkit, Apache-2.0), a Go binary, v0.9.0 of 2026-09-05. The digests are the ones GitHub
# publishes for the release assets (`gh api repos/ocsf/ocsf-toolkit/releases/tags/v0.9.0`), and match its SHA256SUMS.
OCSF_TOOLKIT_VERSION=v0.9.0
OCSF_TOOLKIT_SHA256_AMD64=641fec9f8c457896c06ead9136fe8f4e50753cd95f8a68c24b25c2bcc9cfd5a5
OCSF_TOOLKIT_SHA256_ARM64=d05d95013804eb7238afd6b4bf2478464ec3bd91d38070370c53f37dd96ac7b9
